title 05 | Delivery, delay, and failure [overview stages 5-8]
participant "Client HTTP\n+ TLS process" as C #DBEAFE
participant "Client OS\nTCP sender / receiver" as CT #EDE9FE
participant "Intermediate router\ninput -> queue -> output" as R #FFEDD5
participant "Server OS\nTCP receiver / sender" as ST #EDE9FE
participant "Server TLS\n+ HTTP handler" as S #DBEAFE

note over C,S
  READ FIRST: the connection is already established; these are illustrative byte ranges.
  TCP sequence numbers count stream bytes, including encrypted TLS data in this example.
  SYN/FIN each consume a sequence number. ACK-only segments do not consume stream bytes.
  Network arrows summarize link and physical transmission detailed in 04.
end note

== 5 | Queueing and physical transmission occur at every hop ==
C -> CT: Write request ciphertext into socket send buffer
CT -> CT: Respect available send-buffer space,\ncongestion window, advertised receive window, and pacing
CT -> R: TCP segment carrying encrypted request bytes
R -> R: Receive bits/frame; extract IP packet;\nprocessing and forwarding-table lookup
R -> R: Wait in egress queue behind other packets
R -> R: Transmit packet in outgoing frame\nSerialization time = L / R_link
R -> ST: Signals propagate along the outgoing medium\nPropagation time = distance / signal_speed
note over R #FEF3C7
  Per-hop delay = processing + queueing + transmission + propagation.
  L is transmitted size in bits; R_link is outgoing bit rate in bits/second.
  Higher bit rate reduces transmission time, not propagation distance.
  Queueing varies with competing traffic; a full queue can drop arrivals.
  Throughput is constrained by bottlenecks and sharing along the path.
end note

== 5-6 | Loss and ordering: an independent worked TCP example ==
note over CT,ST #EDE9FE
  For this example, server next expected byte is 1000.
  Each illustrated segment carries 1000 stream bytes.
end note
CT -> ST: Seq=1000, payload bytes 1000..1999
ST --> CT: Cumulative ACK=2000 (next expected byte)
CT -> R: Seq=2000, payload bytes 2000..2999
R -> R: Segment dropped: output queue full
CT -> ST: Seq=3000, payload bytes 3000..3999
ST -> ST: Buffer out-of-order bytes 3000..3999\nDo not deliver across missing byte range
ST --> CT: Duplicate ACK=2000; optional SACK for 3000..3999
note over CT,ST
  Enough duplicate ACKs or another loss detector can trigger earlier recovery.
  One duplicate ACK alone does not imply classic fast retransmit.
  Here we illustrate retransmission on timeout instead.
end note
CT -> CT: Retransmission timer expires\nReduce congestion window according to sender's algorithm
CT -> ST: Retransmit Seq=2000, bytes 2000..2999
ST -> ST: Gap filled; stream now contiguous through byte 3999
ST --> CT: ACK=4000
ST --> S: Socket read provides newly contiguous ciphertext bytes
S -> S: Assemble/verify TLS records; decrypt;\nparse complete HTTP request; run handler
note over ST,S
  ACK=4000 says bytes before 4000 reached TCP in order.
  It does not say TLS accepted them, the handler ran, or a database commit succeeded.
  Late duplicates are recognized by sequence numbers and not delivered twice by TCP.
end note

== 7 | Server response is also a reliable byte stream ==
S -> ST: Encrypt HTTP response; write ciphertext bytes
ST -> CT: Response segments on the same full-duplex connection
CT --> ST: ACKs; advertise receive-buffer capacity
CT --> C: Ordered stream bytes -> TLS -> HTTP response

== Flow control and congestion control operate together ==
note over CT,ST #EDE9FE
  FLOW CONTROL: peer's receive window (rwnd) protects its receive buffers.
  CONGESTION CONTROL: sender's congestion window (cwnd) limits network load.
  Outstanding bytes are bounded approximately by min(rwnd, cwnd);
  allowance for new data also subtracts bytes already in flight.
  A slow-reading application can shrink rwnd and cause backpressure.
  cwnd changes with ACK progress, detected loss, and algorithm-specific signals.
  TCP checksum detects many accidental errors; TLS supplies cryptographic integrity.
end note
note over R,ST #DCFCE7
  Corrupt link frame: FCS failure usually drops it before TCP sees it.
  Wi-Fi may retry locally; ordinary full-duplex Ethernet has no equivalent per-frame ACK.
  IP offers best-effort delivery; eventual TCP recovery is performed by endpoints.
  Recovery is not guaranteed forever: connection or application deadlines can expire.
end note

== Failure scenarios | Separate examples, not additional successful stages ==
group Before HTTP data can be exchanged
  note over C,S
    DNS NXDOMAIN / timeout / SERVFAIL: fail or retry resolution [02].
    TCP refusal: remote RST; silent filtering/path failure: connect timeout.
    TLS identity or handshake failure: abort the secure connection [03].
  end note
end
group During packet forwarding
  note over CT,R #FFEDD5
    No usable route: drop; ICMP unreachable may be returned when permitted.
    TTL expires: drop; ICMP Time Exceeded may be returned.
    IPv4 DF packet exceeds MTU: drop; ICMP fragmentation-needed may be returned.
    Sender adapts with path MTU discovery; blocked ICMP can cause a black hole.
    Probe-based discovery can provide another way to find usable packet sizes.
  end note
end
group After request transmission
  note over C,S
    TLS authentication-tag failure: terminate the secure connection.
    HTTP 4xx/5xx: networking can succeed while the application reports an error.
    Timeout/reset after sending: the client may not know whether the handler committed work.
    Application retries need suitable semantics / idempotency to avoid duplicate side effects.
  end note
end

== 8 | Reuse saves setup; a timing model explains first-byte latency ==
note over C,S
  Cold, simplified first-byte time:
  DNS time + 1 RTT for TCP + 1 RTT for full TLS 1.3 + 1 RTT for HTTP + server processing.
  Assumes immediate handshake progress, no loss/retry, and a small request/first response bytes.
  Network configuration may add earlier delay; full response transfer adds further time.
  Reused TCP/TLS connections avoid those connection-establishment round trips.
  RTT = round-trip time. Close/reuse sequencing is in 03; HTTP/3 differs in 06.
end note
legend left
  |= Color |= Role |
  |<#DBEAFE> Application | HTTP, DNS, application logic |
  |<#FCE7F3> Security | TLS above TCP; SSL is the historical name |
  |<#EDE9FE> Transport | TCP; sockets are a local API boundary |
  |<#FFEDD5> Network | IP addressing and router forwarding |
  |<#DCFCE7> Link | Ethernet / Wi-Fi framing and local delivery |
  |<#FEF3C7> Physical | Electrical, optical, or radio signals |
  Solid arrows: actions / exchanges. Dashed arrows: replies / completions.
  Arrow style does not imply a different delivery guarantee.
endlegend

@enduml
